 ### Parameters and Edge Cases
-- **Grid Size:** The sources specify a regular grid of 100 sub-volumes in the simulation [S1]. The method relies on equal-sized cubic sub-volumes to simplify neighbor identification [S1].
-- **View Direction:** The obstacle-flags are view-dependent [S1]. If the camera moves, flags must be regenerated [S1].
-- **Precision:** The method assumes binary visibility (obscured or not) based on grid topology, not ray-casting precision [S1]. This is a simplification for scheduling efficiency [S1].
-- **Memory:** The method avoids storing results of non-combinable sub-volumes in memory on the central manager, reducing memory pressure [S1].
+- **Sub-Volume Size:** Equally sized cubic sub-volumes are used to allow flexible screen placement and easy obstacle-flag implementation [S1].
+- **View Direction:** Critical for determining obscuration. The flags are generated based on the current view [S1].
+- **Precision:** The method relies on integer bitmasks, avoiding floating-point precision issues in scheduling logic [S1].
+- **Memory:** Non-combinable sub-volumes are not stored in memory on the central manager until they become combinable, reducing memory usage compared to storing all results [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The scheduling overhead is low, as it involves simple bitmask operations and neighbor lookups [S1]. The total rendering time is reduced compared to sequential scheduling because agents work in parallel without idle waiting periods [S1].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of sub-volumes, as each sub-volume stores a small fixed-size flag (4 or 6 bits) [S1].
-- **Performance:** In a simulation with heterogeneous agents and a regular grid of 100 sub-volumes, the method showed significant efficiency improvements over sequential scheduling [S1]. The improvement is attributed to the ability to dispatch jobs to faster agents immediately when they become available, rather than waiting for a strict sequence [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Handles heterogeneous agent speeds well; reduces idle time; scalable to large grids [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Requires a grid structure; visibility is determined by grid topology rather than actual ray-casting occlusion, which may lead to sub-optimal scheduling in complex scenes with internal transparency [S1]. The method assumes front-to-back compositing [S1].
+- **Time Complexity:** The scheduling overhead is low, as it involves simple bit manipulation and neighbor checks [S1]. The overall rendering time is reduced compared to sequential scheduling due to better utilization of heterogeneous agents [S1].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is minimized because only combinable (visible) sub-volumes are processed and stored in the final image accumulation. Intermediate results for obscured sub-volumes are not held in memory [S1].
+- **Performance:** Experiments on a university campus grid showed significant improvements in efficiency for large-scale volume rendering compared to sequential job-scheduling [S1].
+- **Strengths:**
